  "source_text": "How a remembered goal becomes a new command\ni\n\nWellness ranks remembered states. The planner searches for a new command from the body's current state. The outcome is measured again.\n\nPlanner execution on \u00b7 Weight updates enabled \u00b7 Future wellness not an objective of command search\n\n1\nChoose a remembered state\ni\n\nKeep recent memories of what the body sensed and its wellness. Pick a valued memory; its sensory readings become the goal.\n\nThe goal is a state to approach, not an old command to repeat.\n\n2\nSearch for a new command\ni\n\nStart from the current state and controller command. Adjust the command to make predicted senses match the goal, with a cost for effort.\n\nThen a separate hand search aims at the remembered hand pose.\n\n3\nExecute the final command\ni\n\nThe planner can supply the next command. Other commands can come from the controller, with exploration when enabled. Smoothing and a command hold affect what reaches the body.\n\nLearning receives the command actually executed.\n\n4\nMeasure what happened\ni\n\nRead the resulting senses and calculate wellness again. Record predicted versus measured change for the five wellness terms that have predictions.\n\nReaching the remembered state need not bring back its old wellness.\n\nOutcome \u2192 future goal choices\ni\n\nStore the new sensory readings and measured wellness in the goal ring. Updated channel statistics affect the next ranking.\n\nOutcome \u2192 lessons for the model\ni\n\nUse previous senses and the executed command to predict the arriving senses. Prediction errors train the model; the learned wellness copy has its own matching lesson.\n\nDoes the command bring wellness back? That must be measured. Fatigue, contact and recent learning can change its value. The recorded wellness comparison does not directly reward a command or penalize a missed goal.\n\nTiming, scoring and weight updates",
